  by: agent  # fa25
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fa25
  clock: 4  # fa25
  nonce: fa25  # fa25
  set:  # fa25
    description: "Sibling of WI-zigah-fanob (Python Level 1). Each non-Python analyzer likely has its own dotted-name / namespace-aliased / star-import call-resolution gap analogous to the Python dotted-submodule bug. Audit and fix per-language as a later cohort of work, once Level 1 (Python) and Level 2 (NameMatcher utility) have shipped.\n\nLanguages to audit (non-exhaustive):\n- JavaScript/TypeScript: 'import { get } from axios' vs 'import axios' vs 'import * as ax from axios' — three source-form spellings of the same callable. Each should produce a calls edge.\n- Go: aliased package imports ('import h net/http' + 'h.Get()'), dot imports ('. net/http' + 'Get()').\n- Rust: 'use std::env::consts::OS' vs 'use std::env' + 'env::consts::OS' vs fully qualified 'std::env::consts::OS'. WI-vipur fixed some cases for the tree-sitter analyzer; the rust-analyzer backend and other cases likely remain.\n- Java: wildcard imports ('import java.util.*' + bare type use), static imports ('import static X.method'), nested class references.\n- C++: 'using namespace std;' + 'cout' (tracked under WI-zojid). Other namespace-aliased cases.\n- Ruby: namespaced constants ('include Foo::Bar'), explicit fully-qualified calls.\n- Elixir: aliased modules ('alias Foo.Bar' + 'Bar.baz()'), import with only: / except:.\n\nMethod: adopt the polyglot coverage fixture from Level 2 (WI-pisab-furis) as the audit instrument. Each language's fixture enumerates top-N import/call constructs; property test asserts the analyzer emits a calls edge for every call site. Failing cases → per-language work items (similar to WI-dulun for C, WI-bunul for Java, WI-dadid for Elixir already done).\n\n## Current state (2026-05-14) — UNBLOCKED ITERATIVELY\n\nWI-pisab-furis (Level 2) shipped in full on 2026-04-23 across four PRs (#3313, #3315, #3316, #3317, #3318):\n- NameMatcher utility and matcher-boundary canonicalization patterns are live and language-agnostic.\n- AST-walking lint converts containment from convention to enforcement.\n- ADR-0028 stabilized the pass criterion (`edge.is_resolved == True` with an evidence_type drawn from `evidence_types_on_axis(AXIS_INFERENCE_PATHWAY)`).\n- The polyglot call-site coverage fixture exists FOR PYTHON ONLY. Each non-Python language needs its own fixture file authored using the Python file as the template before the audit can run on it.\n\nImplementation path per non-Python language (iterative, half-day each):\n1. Author the fixture file (~5 idiomatic import × call constructs for that language) using the Phase 4 Python fixture as a template.\n2. Run the property test; misses surface as failing assertions.\n3. If misses surface: file a per-language analyzer-fix WI item (sibling shape: WI-dulun / WI-bunul / WI-dadid).\n\n## Scope collision worth resolving before start\n\nWI-nigah (WI-mafik Phase 2 umbrella, `needs_human_review`) lists \"Extend polyglot fixture to 5-10 additional languages\" as one of its children. That child IS this item's prerequisite. Before either starts, decide which umbrella owns fixture-extension — likely WI-mafik, since the audit-and-fix loop here drives the fixture's purpose, while WI-nigah's other children (bakeoff-validation, sweep-style dst_ref retrofit) are downstream of the audit's findings."  # fa25
